Z||000000||FieroJoe||04-08-2001||12:08 AM||||Something that I'll eventually need to know, especially now since I am almost done with the wetsanding to repaint my coupe.  What color is the trunk lock supposed to be on notchbacks?  I dont like the look of it painted black like it is right now.<P>Which way does it come from the factory?  Just bare metal, or painted same color of car?<P>------------------<BR>AIM: Snowboarder0311<BR>87 Coupe 5sp. - big resto in progress<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||rogergarrison||04-08-2001||12:58 AM||||Same as in engine compartment, Krylon semi-flat black is a good match. I have seen some silver (maybe the paint just came off) I have bare silver on mine because the cylinder is out of a cavalier, orig was too thick to fit the hole in my kits trunklid.<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 04-08-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||04-08-2001||03:38 AM||||Stock they were black, Replacements from the Fiero Store are unpainted (silver) I never looked into buying a black one thru an auto parts store or dealer.||||reg||1
Z||000003||FieroJoe||04-08-2001||06:10 PM||||hmm, I had mine black, and it's already chipping off.  I dont like the look of it, I think that it sticks out too much.  I think I'll just paint it blue when I paint the car.<P>Thanks for the info!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>AIM: Snowboarder0311<BR>87 Coupe 5sp. - big resto in progress<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||04-08-2001||06:16 PM||||Locks take a beating. The silver finsh shows it less. The black may look beter depending on what color you paint the car.<P>Don't forget tor soak it good with WD-40 or something like that every so often. Especially if you mostly will use a power release. Spraying it once a year will keep it from seizing up.<P>unless you are competing in tome super stock classs I'd use the silver finish.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 04-08-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||FieroJoe||04-08-2001||06:22 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by theogre:<BR><B>Don't forget tor soak it good with WD-40 or something like that every so often. Especially if you mostly will use a power release. Spraying it once a year will keep it from seizing up.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I use a product called Lock-ease on all my auto locks and house locks.  It is a spray that has graphite in a liquid carrier that evaporates.  Works well, I use it often.<P><P>------------------<BR>AIM: Snowboarder0311<BR>87 Coupe 5sp. - big resto in progress<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
